Description: Let Echo and Reverb ring out instead of being cut off when disabled
 Stock Mixxx deliberately clears Echo's delay buffer and ramps Reverb's send
 to zero over a single audio callback the instant an effect unit is disabled
 (EchoEffect::processChannel and ReverbEffect::processChannel, both keyed on
 EffectEnableState::Disabling). That happens even though the underlying
 EngineEffectChain::process() already stops calling every effect in the unit
 after that one callback regardless, so on a physical CDJ/DJM-style "ring out"
 the tail is lost either way.
 .
 This patch adds an opt-in tail: EffectManifest gains tailTimeMillis (default
 0, i.e. every existing effect keeps today's instant cutoff unchanged).
 EchoEffect and ReverbEffect set it to 6000 ms and drop their explicit
 clear-buffer-on-Disabling code, so their own feedback/damping math decays
 the sound naturally once real input stops.
 .
 EngineEffectChain::process() is extended, per input/output channel pair, so
 that once a channel would normally go from Disabling to fully Disabled, it
 instead keeps calling every effect in that chain for up to the chain's
 longest tailTimeMillis, then stops for good exactly as before. Only what
 gets FED INTO the effects during that window is silenced (a separate
 pEffectFeedIn, not the real pIn), so no new signal keeps the tail going;
 the channel's own dry volume in the final mix stays completely normal
 throughout, exactly as if the effect were simply off. (v2: the first
 version of this patch silenced the final mix's dry term too, not just the
 effect feed. Since switching which channel an effect unit is focused on
 disables the previous channel's routing through exactly the same
 Disabling path as turning the whole unit off, that bug made a channel's
 own volume disappear for the entire tail window - not just the effect's -
 whenever focus moved away from it while an effect with a tail was loaded.
 Fixed by keeping the final dry+wet mix on the real pIn.) This is pure
 bookkeeping on top of the existing enableState machinery: the real
 enableState/m_enableState fields, and the ordinary Enabling/Disabling/
 fadeout paths, are untouched, so every other effect and every other effect
 rack (including the quick-effect racks) behaves exactly as it does in stock
 Mixxx; only a chain that contains an effect with tailTimeMillis > 0 (Echo or
 Reverb) gets the extra callbacks, and only for the channel(s) that were just
 disabled.
Author: GRV6 build (local)
Forwarded: no

